(* ============================================================================
   17. A FIGURE MAY WRAP A TABLE, AND NO CARVE SOURCE SPELLS IT -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-036].
      `figure.target` admits a `table`, and a `table` carries its own optional
      `caption`. Those are two trees, not two spellings of one, and both render:

        figure{ target: table, caption }  ->  <figure>
                                                <table>...</table>
                                                <figcaption>...</figcaption>
                                              </figure>

        table{ caption }                  ->  <table>
                                                <caption>...</caption>
                                                ...
                                              </table>

      PART 9 §4 rules the SOURCE spelling, and it produces only the second: a
      `^ ` line after a table attaches to the table, so a parser never produces
      the first. The producer is a format bridge -- an HTML importer reading
      `<figure><table>…<figcaption>`, a shape HTML itself distinguishes from a
      table carrying a `<caption>`.

      CARVE 0.1 SOURCE HAS NO SPELLING FOR THE WRAPPER. Like `shortCaption` in
      §14 and `rowGroups` in §15, it enters a tree through an AST consumer or a
      format bridge and survives AST encode/decode by §6. Being able to
      REPRESENT the wrapper is not being able to AUTHOR one.

      A CANONICAL CARVE WRITER EMITS THE TARGET AND ITS CAPTION -- the table
      followed by the `^ ` line -- and MUST NOT invent source syntax for the
      wrapper. What it writes re-reads as `table{caption}`, a different tree
      with different HTML, so the wrapper is LOST rather than preserved. A
      bridge or API which exposes conversion diagnostics SHOULD report that
      structural loss. This is the one caption shape whose source round trip
      changes the rendering, which is why it is written down here rather than
      left to each writer to discover.

      THE TARGET'S OWN CAPTION SLOT MAY ALREADY BE FULL. A `table` carries an
      optional `caption`, so `figure{ target: table{ caption }, caption }` is a
      schema-valid tree with TWO captions and only one `^ ` slot to write them
      into. The wrapper's caption takes that slot only when the target has none;
      where the target has its own, the figure's caption is LOST WITH THE
      WRAPPER, exactly as the rest of the wrapper is, and the diagnostic above
      covers it. A WRITER MUST NOT EMIT A SECOND `^ ` LINE for the figure's
      caption. There is no second slot for it to land in: a `^ ` line after a
      caption line is not a caption at all, it is an ordinary paragraph, so the
      output would carry the caption text as literal body prose instead of
      losing it cleanly, and the loss would stop being observable as a loss.

      A FIGURE GROUP'S TABLE PANEL IS NOT THIS WRAPPER. §16 holds a
      `figure_group`'s panels in `children` as exactly the nodes the inner
      caption rules built, so a table panel is a plain `table` child of the
      group -- carrying its own `caption` if it has one -- and never a `figure`
      whose target is a table. A group wraps a SEQUENCE of panels and captions
      the sequence, while this wrapper wraps ONE table and captions that table.
      Carve 0.1 source still spells no `figure{target: table}`, which is what
      makes the writer's loss unavoidable rather than a choice.

      THE TABLE IS THE ONLY HOST IN THIS POSITION. PART 9 §4b states what a
      caption makes of its host: every other captionable host -- an image, a
      quote, a code block, a display-math paragraph -- becomes a `figure` when
      a `^ ` line follows it, so its wrapper IS what the source spells and the
      round trip is exact. A table is the one host whose caption has a slot of
      its own to go into, so the wrapper around it says something the caption
      inside it does not, and only that difference is unspellable.

   18. A CITATION DEFINITION IS A NODE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-037].
      `type: "citation_definition"`, carrying:
        key       the citation key as the author wrote it, without the `@`
        children  the entry's inline content: what follows the `]: ` separator
                  and the optional metadata block, parsed as inline
        attrs     the leading `{author= year=}` metadata block, when the
                  definition carries one
      and its `pos` under §4, like every other node.

      SHAPED AFTER §10's LINK REFERENCE DEFINITION, NOT AFTER THE FOOTNOTE.
      That is the closer of the two analogues: a `footnote` holds BLOCKS,
      while `[@key]: {author= year=} entry` holds a metadata run plus one line
      of rendered text. So the entry is `children` of INLINE nodes, and the
      metadata lands in `attrs` -- the same two slots §10 spends on a
      definition line's tail, with `key` where that clause has `label`. `key`
      rather than `label` because `citation.key` already names the same string
      at the use site, and §3 makes field names spec surface: one construct
      identified two ways in one document is the collision §1 exists to stop.

      NO RENDERED OUTPUT MOVES ON ANY TARGET. The node renders nothing where it
      sits; the entry's text renders in the references list the extension
      builds. Conformance is asserted at the AST level.

      TIER-2, SO THE NODE EXISTS ONLY WHERE CITATIONS ARE ENABLED. With the
      extension off, `[@key]: entry` is not a citation definition and it is not
      a reference definition either -- the leading `@` is reserved against that
      in core -- so the line is ordinary paragraph text. A default-profile parse
      therefore never produces this type, which is why the schema declares it
      and the core corpus cannot reach it.

      WHERE A DEFINITION IS RECOGNIZED IS NOT DECIDED HERE. §10 hoists a link
      reference definition out of a container while §7 rules that an
      abbreviation definition inside one is not a definition at all, and this
      clause is about the node's SHAPE rather than about which lines produce
      one. An implementation emitting the node for a definition it recognizes
      satisfies this clause whichever way that question lands.

   18a. A CITATION ITEM IS A POSITIONED INLINE NODE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-038].
      Every item in `citation_group.items` carries `type: "citation"` and the
      `pos` of PART 12 §4, spanning its optional prefix, `-` author
      suppression marker, `@key`, locator and suffix. Its existing `key`,
      `prefix`, `locator`, `locatorLabel`, `locatorValue`, `suffix`,
      `suppressAuthor`, `number` and `useIndex` fields are unchanged.

      The item is content, not bookkeeping: an editor navigates to a locator,
      a diagnostic points at one key among several, and an AST transform may
      replace one citation without replacing its group. It therefore follows
      the same typed, positioned node contract as every other content-bearing
      value in the tree and belongs to the inline profile vocabulary.

      §4 GOVERNS THE POSITION, AS IT DOES EVERYWHERE ELSE. "The `pos` of §4" is
      that clause whole, exemptions included: a node the producer could not
      place omits the field, and a synthesized one MUST omit it. The schema
      read this sentence as a hard requirement and marked `pos` required on
      `citation` alone, which left a citation an importer or an editing API
      built with no conformant encoding at all - omitting the field failed the
      schema, and supplying one violated §4. A parser still places every
      citation it reads; nothing about a parsed tree moves
      ([carve#2192](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/issues/2192)).

   19. A TABLE MAY CARRY OPTIONAL POSITIONAL COLUMN METADATA -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-039]. A
      `table` MAY carry `columns`, an array whose entry at index N describes
      column N. An entry MAY carry:

        align   one of `left`, `right` or `center`
        valign  one of `top`, `middle` or `bottom`
        width   a number greater than zero and at most one: the column's
                FRACTION of the table width

      Either field may be absent, meaning unset. The array MAY be empty or
      shorter than the widest row; a missing entry means both fields are unset
      for that column. Widths need not sum to one. A sum above one is a
      diagnostic concern for a source spelling or format bridge, not an invalid
      exchange tree.

      RESOLUTION IS PER FIELD. A cell's own value wins, then the corresponding
      column value, then unset. One explicit cell field does not suppress a
      different field inherited from the column. This keeps the rule usable
      when a later version adds another alignment axis.

      SOURCE SPELLING. A table's preceding block-attribute line MAY carry
      `aligns`, `valigns` and `widths`. Each value is a comma-separated
      positional list; an empty entry means unset. Alignment names are the
      vocabularies above. Widths are numbers greater than zero and at most 100
      in source, and divide by 100 on the way into the fractional AST field;
      any other width is an error. More entries than columns is
      an error. Fewer is valid, leaves the tail unset, and produces a lint
      diagnostic. Widths need not total 100; a total above it lints.

      PRECEDENCE IS LOCALITY, PER FIELD: a cell marker wins, then an in-table
      column marker (native header or GFM delimiter), then the table attribute,
      then unset. Supplying two column-level spellings for one field lints and
      the in-table spelling wins. Existing header markers and delimiter colons
      keep their tree and output; attributes fill only facts they do not state.

      LISTTABLE CELL SPELLING. Because a ListTable cell is a list item, its
      marker attribute MAY carry `align=left|right|center` and/or
      `valign=top|middle|bottom`. These values populate the corresponding
      `table_cell` fields and override the positional column value independently.
      They are consumed as semantics and MUST NOT also appear as literal HTML
      `align` or `valign` attributes.

      THE WRITER RETAINS THE MOST LOCAL AVAILABLE SPELLING. A headed table
      keeps native column markers. A headerless aligned table emits `aligns`.
      Widths emit percentages with trailing zeroes removed. Metadata a target
      cannot spell is omitted and reported by conversion diagnostics.

      HTML applies resolved horizontal and vertical alignment to each cell,
      with `text-align` before `vertical-align`; widths emit a `colgroup` and
      one `col` per entry. Markdown keeps only column horizontal alignment where
      it can emit a delimiter row. Plain and ANSI ignore all three fields.

   20. TABLE ALIGNMENT RUNS CARRY TWO INDEPENDENT AXES -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-040]. A cell
      alignment run is glued to its opening pipe, after an optional `=` kind
      marker, and is terminated by exactly one space after an optional glued
      attribute block. Horizontal markers are `<`, `~`, `>`; vertical markers
      are `^`, `~`, `v` for top, middle and bottom. A horizontal marker may
      stand alone; a vertical marker MUST follow a horizontal marker or the
      `?` inheritance placeholder. Vertical-then-horizontal order is invalid.
      `~~` means center plus middle. Any other
      duplicate axis is invalid and falls back visibly rather than consuming
      a prefix. The only valid order is horizontal (`x`) then vertical (`y`).

      A lone `~` is horizontal center. `?` is the horizontal-inheritance
      placeholder and is valid only before a vertical marker: `?^`, `?~` and
      `?v` contribute no cell-level `align`, so that field resolves from the
      column while the run contributes `top`, `middle` or `bottom` as the
      cell-level `valign`. A lone `?`, a reversed vertical-plus-`?` pair and
      every other `?` run are ordinary visible content. A header-cell run supplies
      column defaults; a body-cell run supplies only that cell. A run with no
      following space is not alignment: this deliberately changes the glued
      `|>text` family so marker runs have an unambiguous terminator. `lint`
      reports the old glued form and `fmt --migrate` inserts the space.

      `table_cell` carries optional `valign` beside `align`, with the same
      inheritance rule. A whitespace-delimited cell containing only `^` or `<`
      remains a rowspan/colspan marker; gluing is what distinguishes alignment.

   21. U+0000 IS REPLACED ON INGEST -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-041]. A reader
      replaces every U+0000 with U+FFFD REPLACEMENT CHARACTER in every string
      value it ingests. It does this BEFORE any other reading of the value -
      before a sentinel is looked for in it, before it is used as a key,
      before it reaches a renderer.

      THE SUBJECT IS THE DECODED VALUE, not the bytes of a JSON document. A
      string reaches an ingest by two doors and the rule is the same at both:
      decoded from JSON text, where the only spelling that can carry this
      character is the `\u0000` escape, and handed in directly by a host that
      built the tree in memory and called the ingest on it. This clause does
      NOT relax the JSON grammar: RFC 8259 forbids an unescaped U+0000 inside
      a string, so a raw byte in JSON text is a syntax error before any Carve
      rule is reached, and stays one. It is named here only because the second
      door has no JSON layer to raise it.

      THIS MIRRORS THE PARSE BOUNDARY, which is the whole of the rule: PART 0
      INPUT performs the same replacement on source, and PART 9 §29 carves
      U+0000 out of the content class on that basis. An AST is a second door
      into the same renderers; it takes the same doormat.

      A READER DOES NOT REFUSE THE VALUE.
      §11 and §12 refuse an unknown property and a deviant root, because those
      are structure a producer got wrong. This is different: the replacement is
      what the parse boundary already does to the identical string, so
      performing it is the documented reading rather than a repair. Refusing
      would make an ingested document stricter than the same document written
      as source.

      AN IMPORTER IS THE SAME BOUNDARY BY THE SAME REASONING, and is stated
      here as a SHOULD rather than a MUST because the format being imported may
      have its own rule.

   22. AN INGESTED VALUE THE SCHEMA CALLS ABSENT IS NORMALIZED AWAY --
      NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-042]. `resources/ast-schema.json` describes a list's
      `start` as "First number of an ordered list, when it is not 1". That
      sentence is a statement about what a CONFORMANT TREE looks like, and an
      encoder is the thing that has to honor it. So when an ingested tree
      carries `start: 1`, the encoder MUST NOT re-emit it: the field is dropped,
      and every tree the implementation publishes matches the documented shape.

      §6's round trip is scoped to `parse(x)`, whose tree never carries
      `start: 1`; it does not promise JSON-to-JSON identity for an ingested
      payload no parser produced.

      THE DECIDING ASYMMETRY IS THAT NORMALIZING IS LOSSLESS. `start: 1` and no
      `start` describe the same document, so nothing an author wrote is
      discarded, while preserving the value makes the encoder's output depend
      on WHERE THE TREE CAME FROM.

      The encoder accepts this payload: `start` is schema-named and in range;
      only its default value is non-canonical.

      This does not drop `start` generally: `start: 0` and `start: 2` pass
      through unchanged.

   23. A BLOCK IMAGE IS A NAMED FIELD ON THE PARAGRAPH -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-043]
      (PART 9R R7 owns the phase; this clause owns the wire).

      A paragraph the promotion phase promoted carries `blockImage: true`:

        {"type":"paragraph","blockImage":true,
         "children":[{"type":"image","src":"/u","alt":"a",
                      "ref":"r","rawRef":"[r]"}]}

      The field is set by that phase and by NOTHING ELSE. It is present only as
      `true`; a paragraph that is not a block image omits it rather than
      carrying `false`, which is §22's absent-value rule applied here.

      IT IS NOT A NON-ANCHOR FLAG. §3a refuses a flag on a nested link because
      that flag would carry a RENDER fact -- "HTML cannot nest anchors" -- back
      into a format that describes the source. This field carries a RESOLUTION
      fact, which §3a already publishes, and it is set by the resolution phase
      rather than by one render target's limitation.

      NOT A DISTINCT NODE TYPE, deliberately. `block_image` is the honest shape
      -- it IS a different construct -- but it breaks every consumer that
      switches on node type and it ripples through the engines, the importers,
      `fmt` and this schema at once. A field is additive, and a type stays
      available later.

      AN INGESTED `blockImage` VALUE IS AUTHORITATIVE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-044]. A reader that receives
      `blockImage` USES it. It runs the promotion phase itself only for a tree
      that LACKS the field. Recomputing it on every ingest makes the field
      advisory and re-introduces the re-derivation this clause removes.

      ABSENCE IS NOT A CLAIM. It means the producer did not run the phase --
      every tree serialized before this clause lacks the field -- so a reader
      MUST NOT read absence as "this paragraph is ordinary", and MUST NOT
      reject a tree for omitting it. Requiring it was considered and refused:
      it breaks every existing AST JSON document for no benefit, and §11's
      strict-ingest surface is about properties the schema does not NAME, not
      about ones it names optionally.

      `fmt` WRITES THE SOURCE SPELLING, NOT `blockImage` -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-045]. `fmt` writes the SOURCE SPELLING,
      so promotion is not observable in `fmt` output and a document whose
      reference is never defined still round-trips to itself (§6). The
      alternative makes the writer non-local: the same paragraph would be
      written differently depending on whether a definition appears somewhere
      else in the document.

      AN IMPORTER READS THE FIELD RATHER THAN GUESSING. An HTML importer that
      escapes a following `^ ` line because the image above it "would capture
      it" is deriving R7 a third time, in a pipeline with no access to the
      phase (`docs/html-import.md`, the detached-caption case). It reads
      `blockImage` on the tree it built instead.

   24. A CHILDREN ARRAY HOLDS ONLY A CONTENT BLOCK -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-046].
      Nine fields in `resources/ast-schema.json` hold a list of block nodes,
      and all nine are spelled `children`: on `document`, `admonition`,
      `block_quote`, `definition_description`, `div`, `figure_group`,
      `footnote`, `line_block` and `list_item`. The types they admit are the
      CONTENT blocks. A CONTAINER-INTERNAL type is not among them.

      `table_row`, `table_cell`, `list_item`, `definition_term` and
      `definition_description` exist only inside the container that owns them.
      `table.rows`, `table_row.cells`, `list.items` and
      `definition_list.items` name each of them directly, so a `children`
      array holding one is a tree no parser produces and no renderer has a
      case for.

      THE UNION IS THE ENFORCEMENT. §12(d) refuses a payload by validating it
      against the schema, so a union listing a type makes that type valid
      wherever the union appears -- and the block union listed all five. A
      document whose only child was a `table_row` therefore validated, and the
      engines split on it: carve-js accepted it, carve-rs refused it with a
      rule written in its decoder that the schema does not state, and no
      ledger could declare the divergence because a schema check cannot see a
      shape the schema permits. Narrowing the union is what makes §12(d) able
      to answer, rather than adding a sixth leniency list beside it
      ([carve#2189](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/issues/2189)).

      A `list_item` REACHES ITS OWN CHILDREN THE SAME WAY. It holds content
      blocks, so a nested `list` is a child of an item and a nested
      `list_item` is not -- which is what the source already says.

   25. A REFERENCE NODE CARRIES ITS TARGET -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-047]. A node
      whose whole purpose is to point at something names what it points at,
      and the schema marks that field `required`. `link` requires `href`,
      `image` requires `src`, `heading_ref` requires `target`, `footnote`
      requires `label`.

      `footnote_ref` did not, so `{"type":"footnote_ref"}` validated: a
      reference to nothing, which §3a cannot have produced -- a pre-resolve
      tree records what the author wrote, and nobody writes a footnote
      reference with no label. Resolution results stay optional, as §5 has
      them: `number` arrives when the document resolves the reference and is
      absent when nothing does
      ([carve#2193](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/issues/2193)).

      AND IT IS SPELLED `label`, THE NAME ITS DEFINITION USES. A `footnote`
      carries `label` and so does a `footnote_ref`, for the identical string.
      §7 settles that name on the definition and
      `link_reference_definition` spells it the same way, so the reference
      follows rather than the other way round. `id` is the worse of the two
      names here in any case, because every node ALSO carries `attrs.id`, a
      different value entirely: an authored `{#x}` rather than a lookup key,
      so one name would stand for two things on the same object.

      AN ENGINE MAY KEEP DECODING `id`. That is §11's narrow exception
      unchanged -- a property an implementation once published, decoded onto a
      field the schema does name -- and `footnote.id` is the case that clause
      was written for. The exception covers READING a stored tree; a producer
      emits `label`.

   26. A SPANNING CELL PUBLISHES ITS RESOLVED EXTENT -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-048].
      A `table_cell` that the span walk of PART 9 §13 T5 extended carries
      `colspan`, `rowspan`, or both: the number of columns and rows the cell
      occupies once every `<` and `^` marker that reached it has been consumed.
      They sit on the ORIGIN cell. Absent means 1, and an ingested 1 is
      normalized away on the reasoning §22 gives for a list's `start`.

      THE MARKERS STAY. A continuation cell keeps its `span`, because that is
      what the author wrote and §3a records the document rather than a
      rendering of it. The counts are ADDED ALONGSIDE, the same rule that lets
      a resolved reference keep `ref` and `rawRef` beside `href` and a resolved
      footnote reference keep its label beside its number (§5).

      A MARKER THAT FOUND NO ORIGIN CONTRIBUTES NOTHING. T5 is a total
      function: a `^` in the first row, a `<` in the first column, or a marker
      every intermediate position of which is consumed renders as an empty
      cell and extends nobody, so no cell's count moves. The marker is still
      published, still carrying `span`.

      WHY IT IS PUBLISHED AT ALL. §5 serializes a resolution result a consumer
      could recompute whenever recomputing it means reimplementing the
      resolution rules, and names a generated heading id, a footnote number and
      a caption number for that reason. The span walk is the largest of the
      set: a NORMATIVE total function with an orphan case and a blocked case,
      which every consumer that renders HTML or reaches a table model with
      integer spans would otherwise implement again, separately, from prose.
      Two consumers doing that is the divergence this part exists to prevent -
      and unlike a heading id, a wrong answer here silently changes the shape
      of the table rather than the text of one anchor.

      THE COUNT IS NOT A SECOND SPELLING OF THE MARKERS. A payload whose
      counts contradict its markers is invalid, in the sense §11 means: the
      walk is a function of the markers, so the two cannot disagree in a tree
      any producer built. A reader is not required to re-run the walk to check.

   27. A TABLE CELL MAY CARRY BLOCK CONTENT INSTEAD OF INLINE -- NORMATIVE
      [CARVE-P12-049]. A `table_cell` carries EITHER `children`, the inline
      content every source-spelled cell has, OR `blocks`, a list of block
      nodes. Exactly one: never both, and never neither.

      Carve 0.1 source has no spelling for a block inside a cell, so a parser
      never produces `blocks`. It exists because the models a bridge reaches
      DO: an HTML `<td>` holds flow content and a Pandoc `Cell` holds
      `[Block]`, so a cell holding a list, two paragraphs, a quotation or a
      code block had nowhere to land and was flattened to its text. A
      canonical Carve writer loses the field and reports the loss, the way it
      already does for `shortCaption` and for a `figure` wrapping a `table`.

      WHY A SECOND FIELD RATHER THAN A WIDER `children`. Widening the inline
      list to admit blocks changes the COMMON case for every consumer and
      every engine, and puts inline and block content in one array where a
      walker would have to test each entry. Two fields cost a walker one
      branch, in the one place block content can appear; one widened field
      costs every reader of every cell.

      THE COUNTS AND THE MARKERS ARE UNAFFECTED. `span`, `colspan`, `rowspan`,
      `align`, `valign` and `attrs` sit on the cell and say nothing about how
      its content is spelled, so §26's resolved extent applies to a block cell
      exactly as it does to an inline one.

      IT IS NOT A PLACE TO PUT A PARAGRAPH A SOURCE CELL ALREADY HOLDS. A
      source-spelled cell publishes `children`. A producer that wrapped that
      inline content in a `paragraph` and published `blocks` would be
      describing the same document two ways, which §1a's coalescing rule
      rejects one layer down for the same reason
      ([carve#2191](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/issues/2191)).

   28. SMALL CAPS SURVIVE INTERCHANGE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-050]. A
      `small_caps` inline node carries `children`, plus optional `attrs` and
      `pos` under the ordinary inline-node rules. It records a semantic
      small-caps distinction supplied by an AST consumer or format bridge.

      CARVE 0.1 SOURCE HAS NO SPELLING FOR THIS WRAPPER. A source parser never
      synthesizes it. The node survives AST encode/decode by §6, and a
      structured exporter MAY map it to its target's equivalent small-caps
      construct.

      HTML renders `<span class="smallcaps">`, merging the node's attributes
      under the ordinary span rules. Plain and ANSI output render the children
      without changing their letter case. PART 11 §8c defines the Markdown
      target.

      A canonical Carve source writer MUST write the children without the
      small-caps wrapper. When the node carries `attrs`, it MUST preserve them
      on an ordinary attributed span around those children. It MUST NOT discard
      the children, change their letter case, or invent small-caps source
      syntax. A bridge or API which exposes conversion diagnostics SHOULD
      report the structural loss. A source spelling can be specified separately
      without changing the interchange node.

   29. A DISPLAY EQUATION MAY CARRY A LABEL AND NUMBER -- NORMATIVE
      [CARVE-P12-051]. A `math` node MAY carry `label`, a non-empty authored
      string with no leading or trailing whitespace, used as its visible
      numbering prefix and counter bucket. The
      cross-reference identifier remains `attrs.id`. The label survives
      resolution unchanged. A resolved labeled display equation also carries
      `number`, a positive integer assigned by PART 9R R5a, unless the math sits
      inside a `figure` whose caption carries a number placeholder and owns the
      number. The number is added beside the label under §5's added-alongside
      rule; it never replaces it. "Inside" means at any depth in a figure's
      target or a `figure_group`'s children. For nested figures and groups, the
      nearest enclosing numbered figure or group owns the node. One numbered
      caption suppresses direct numbering for every display-math descendant in
      its target or children. Those equations retain `label`, but have no
      `number` and do not register their own `attrs.id` values as numbered
      cross-reference targets.

      Only a node carrying `display: true` may carry `number`. An inline math
      node carrying `number` is invalid on ingest. A label on inline math is
      retained for interchange but draws no number and registers no numbered
      cross-reference target. A number without a label is invalid: it has no
      counter bucket or visible prefix.

      Carve 0.1 source has no direct spelling for either field. Parsers do not
      synthesize `label`; the field enters through an AST consumer or format
      bridge. A canonical source writer preserves the formula and reports the
      unspellable label and number through its conversion diagnostics. An
      exporter MAY map the pair to its target's equation label and numbering
      model.

   30. SECTIONING IS INTERCHANGE-ONLY -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-052]. A `section`
      carries `children`, the blocks it encloses, and MAY carry `level`, the
      heading level the source format stated for it.

      Carve source spells no section: sectioning is implicit in heading level,
      and PART 9 derives every structure that depends on it from the heading
      run. A parser therefore produces no `section`, no corpus document
      contains one, and a canonical Carve writer flattens one back to its
      headings and reports the loss.

      IT EXISTS BECAUSE THE FORMATS ON THE OTHER SIDE OF A BRIDGE HAVE IT.
      HTML5 `section`, JATS `sec`, DocBook `section`, DITA and TEI all carry
      sectioning explicitly, and an importer reading one had to flatten it into
      headings - discarding nesting that every consumer wanting it then
      re-derived from `heading.level`, separately. That is the same cost §26
      removed for a table's resolved span, one structure up.

      THE LEVEL IS WHAT THE SOURCE SAID, NOT WHAT THE NESTING IMPLIES. A
      format that states a level publishes it; one that does not omits the
      field, and the nesting is the answer. The two cannot disagree in a tree
      an importer built, so a reader is not required to check
      ([carve#2207](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/issues/2207)).

      A CAPTION IS NOT A NODE, and the profile vocabulary no longer says it is.
      `figure`, `figure_group` and `table` each carry their caption as an
      inline array, so there is nothing a profile could deny separately from
      its host. Listing it gave implementations a name that named nothing.

   31. A CITATION ITEM CARRIES ITS OWN MODE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-053].
      A `citation` MAY carry `mode`, its citation mode. Absent means
      parenthetical. `citation_group.mode` is the AUTHORED shorthand that sets
      it: the leading `+` after `[` is the only spelling Carve 0.1 source has,
      and it applies to the whole group, so every item of a source-spelled
      group carries the same value as its group.

      IT SITS ON THE ITEM BECAUSE THE EXCHANGED MODEL PUTS IT THERE. Pandoc's
      `CitationMode` is three-valued and per citation, so a `Cite` mixing an
      author-in-text citation with a parenthetical one had no encoding while
      the mode lived only on the group. The group keeps its field, because
      that is what the author wrote and §3a records the document rather than a
      rendering of it.

      NO CORPUS DOCUMENT MOVES. A parse from Carve source fills every item of
      a group identically from the group's own value, so the only trees where
      the items differ are ones an importer or an editing API built. A
      canonical Carve writer cannot spell such a group and reports the loss.

      THE TWO CANNOT DISAGREE IN A SOURCE-SPELLED TREE, and a reader is not
      required to check. Where they do differ the ITEM is authoritative, since
      it is the finer statement
      ([carve#2203](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/issues/2203)).
   32. RUBY ANNOTATIONS SURVIVE INTERCHANGE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P12-054]. A
      `ruby` inline node carries `pairs`, a non-empty ordered array. Each pair
      is a closed object with exactly `base` and `annotation`: `base` is a
      non-empty array of inline nodes and `annotation` is an array of inline
      nodes which MAY be empty. The ruby node MAY also carry `attrs` and `pos`
      under the ordinary inline-node rules. It has NO parallel `children`
      field; the two arrays are what preserve the association.

      PAIRS ARE STRUCTURE, NOT NODES. They carry no `type`, `attrs` or `pos`.
      Their order is reading order, and every traversal walks `base` before
      `annotation` in pair order. Sanitization, URL policy, profile enforcement,
      resolution, cloning, equality, patching and rendering MUST reach both
      arrays. One `ruby` counts as one inline nesting level. AST ingest still
      measures depth in each implementation's own unit under §9(c).
      The node and every pair MUST survive AST encode/decode under §6.

      CARVE 0.1 SOURCE HAS NO SPELLING FOR RUBY. A source parser never
      synthesizes it. A canonical Carve writer emits every pair as
      `base(annotation)` and reports `ruby-flattened` under PART 2. When the
      node carries `attrs`, the writer wraps the complete fallback in one
      ordinary attributed span. It MUST NOT discard either side or invent a
      source spelling.

      HTML emits one `<ruby>` in pair order, with generated `<rp>(</rp>` and
      `<rp>)</rp>` around each `<rt>`. The outer element receives `attrs`.
      Plain and ANSI emit `base(annotation)` and report `ruby-flattened`.
      PART 11 §8c defines Markdown output.

      PROFILES USE THE EXISTING `disallowedAction`. For `to_text`, the text
      content is each base followed by `(`, its annotation and `)` in pair
      order. `strip` removes the node and subtree; `error` rejects it. Ruby is
      not a SemanticSpan, an `inline_extension` or a span carrying magic
      attributes. A later source spelling MUST map to this node rather than
      introduce a second representation.

   ============================================================================ *)
